  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, skies are sunny. Temperatures are warming through the \n60s and 70s. Surface high pressure is over the region, resulting in \neither light and variable winds (central South Dakota) or 5 to 15 \nmph northwest winds with occasionally higher gusts. The dryness of \nthe air, combined with sunshine, is yielding a drop in relative \nhumidity into the 25 to 40 percent range.\n\nEnsemble clusters analysis plus deterministic model output suggests \nthis upper level ridge over the west/trof over the east flow pattern \nwill persist through tomorrow/Thursday before things start\na-changin'. Thursday night/Friday upper level energy will begin \ncarving out an upper level trof over the west coast that looks to \nstay put for several days, while pumping up an upper level ridge \nover the eastern CONUS. This CWA will see upper level steering winds \ntransition from their current northwest flow orientation to more of \na southwest flow trajectory heading into Friday. The pattern is \nexpected to become a blocking flow pattern (western CONUS Rex-block \nconfiguration) by next Monday, at which point, won't be putting much \nfaith in any solution the models come up with for Monday through the \nend of the forecast period, as models (including ensembles \nclustering) tend to not be able to resolve Rex block patterns all \nthat well. There is usually quite a large range in potential \noutcomes generated by numerical models once a blocking pattern \nenters into potential flow pattern modeling.\n\nSuffice it to say, temperatures will be quite warm (2+ 850hpa \nstandard deviations above normal) for October, this coming Friday \nand Saturday. Then, depending on the timing of it all, a cold front \npushing through the CWA will turn things sharply colder (1-2 850hpa \nstandard deviations below normal) for Sunday and Monday of next \nweek. Sunday and Monday are also when the current precipitation \nchances (widespread 30-55 percent) stand out the most in the \nforecast, when some of this western CONUS upper level trof energy is \nsupposed to lift out onto the central/northern plains.\n\nBeyond that, will be watching fire weather things for the rest of \nthis week into Saturday, as currently forecast afternoon rh values \nover the next few days are expected to be too high. Did\ncollaborate slightly stronger winds/gusts and lower dewpoints on\nSaturday against my east and south neighbors. Saturday could end\nup being a fire weather headline day out ahead of that cold\nfrontal passage. Still plenty of greened up fine fuels across \nthe CWA, what with the marked improvement in drought conditions \n(compare U.S. drought monitor changes between August 18 and \nSeptember 29). But, no more corn sweat to help the boundary \nlayer out. When cold fronts come through this time of year, it \ngets quite dry in the boundary layer in their wake, and \nensemble-based rh forecasts tend to over-represent afternoon rh \nin these season changing regimes. Something to keep an eye on \nfrom today through Saturday.",
